## Marp presentation reference and runtime

The implementation plan for `skills/tool/q-tool-marp` was conceptually informed
by Softaworks' MIT-licensed
[`marp-slide`](https://github.com/softaworks/agent-toolkit/tree/3027f20f3181758385a1bb8c022d4041dfb4de84/skills/marp-slide)
skill at commit `3027f20f3181758385a1bb8c022d4041dfb4de84`
(2026-03-05). Quasar retained only general public ideas about Markdown slide
authoring, Marp directives, image composition, theme CSS, and presentation
quality. The request/result contracts, authority model, offline path and
content security policy, capability probe, renderer, tests, schemas, fixtures,
and all prose are independent Quasar work. No Softaworks source text, script,
CSS, template, or other asset was copied or translated. The neutral and Quasar
themes and templates are original, written from the official Marp/Marpit
specification and, for the branded variant, Quasar's existing presentation
identity. The Softaworks MIT terms and copyright notice appear in their
complete form later in this catalog.

The local runtime pins
[`@marp-team/marp-cli` 4.5.0](https://github.com/marp-team/marp-cli/tree/v4.5.0)
in `package.json` and `package-lock.json`. Marp CLI is Copyright (c) 2018 Marp
team and is licensed under the MIT License. It and its transitive packages are
installed dependencies rather than vendored source; their package-owned
license files remain with them after `npm ci`. The Quasar wrapper never
implicitly installs dependencies or browsers and never authorizes network
access, remote assets, publication, or arbitrary Marp config.

An `npm audit --omit=dev` run on 2026-08-15 reported four high-severity findings
through `@marp-team/marp-cli` -> `puppeteer-core` ->
`@puppeteer/browsers` -> `extract-zip` 2.0.1 for
[`GHSA-jmr9-qjv8-65gv`](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jmr9-qjv8-65gv).
No corrected `extract-zip` release was available in the pinned dependency tree;
the automated proposal was an incompatible Marp CLI downgrade. This repository
records a bounded exception instead: `q-tool-marp` never invokes browser
download or ZIP extraction, accepts no archive input, uses only an already
installed browser after a real local smoke test, and runs offline. The finding
still remains a declared dependency risk and must be reassessed when the pin is
updated.

## Browser-rendered web capture references

The implementation plan for `skills/tool/q-tool-web-markdown` was conceptually
informed by Softaworks' MIT-licensed
[`web-to-markdown`](https://github.com/softaworks/agent-toolkit/blob/3027f20f3181758385a1bb8c022d4041dfb4de84/skills/web-to-markdown/SKILL.md)
skill at commit `3027f20f3181758385a1bb8c022d4041dfb4de84`
(2026-03-05) and by the public browser-backed behavior of MIT-licensed
[`leonardocouy/web2md` 1.2.0](https://github.com/leonardocouy/web2md/tree/b0978b5f4c83b87876588e903d3036370dd27ad7).
Quasar retained only the general idea of manually turning one rendered web page
into Markdown. No upstream source text, code, dependency, package, browser
binary, fixture, asset, or output was copied, translated, vendored, or adapted.

K-Dense's
[`markitdown` skill](https://github.com/K-Dense-AI/scientific-agent-skills/blob/4226ca8052b89b471ab4eedead6e04b0b6aa08ef/skills/markitdown/SKILL.md)
at commit `4226ca8052b89b471ab4eedead6e04b0b6aa08ef` and Microsoft's
MIT-licensed [`MarkItDown` 0.1.6](https://github.com/microsoft/markitdown/tree/v0.1.6)
were consulted only to compare conversion scope and security patterns. Quasar
did not incorporate their document converters, parsers, cloud or plugin routes,
scripts, prose, fixtures, or dependencies.

The shipped runtime is original, dependency-free Quasar code. It uses Node's
standard library to drive an already installed Chrome-family browser through
the Chrome DevTools Protocol and to enforce a connection-pinning local proxy.
It neither invokes nor depends on `web2md` or MarkItDown. Original Quasar
portions remain under the repository MIT license above; the installed browser
and Node runtime retain their own licenses.

## Anthropic PDF functional reference — restricted source not incorporated

Sources consulted:

- <https://github.com/K-Dense-AI/scientific-agent-skills/tree/b2a92ba052dcae4ef48e850b9e31ebf75706be48/skills/pdf>
- <https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/f6656c1256d5a8adfa37db9110046ef20bac644c/skills/pdf>

K-Dense repository commit consulted:
`b2a92ba052dcae4ef48e850b9e31ebf75706be48` (2026-08-14). The PDF
skill identifies metadata version `1.2`, Anthropic as the author, and the
Anthropic repository as its source. Anthropic repository commit
`f6656c1256d5a8adfa37db9110046ef20bac644c` was also consulted on
2026-08-14.

The source skill's own `LICENSE.txt` is not MIT: it governs use through the
applicable Anthropic services agreement and states additional restrictions on
copying, retention, derivative works, distribution, transfer, and reverse
engineering. Those materials were reviewed only to identify the public
functional surface of a PDF capability. No source prose, script, fixture,
asset, form guide, reference guide, or license text was copied, translated,
vendored, or adapted into this repository.

`skills/tool/q-tool-pdf` is an original Quasar implementation. It covers the
same public functional area with an independently designed caller-owned
delegation contract, operation-aware Python/Node routing,
source-preserving atomic output, explicit overwrite authorization, native-tool
security boundaries, runtime-specific references, and deterministic smoke
tests. Original Quasar portions remain under the repository MIT license above;
third-party runtime dependencies retain their own licenses and are not
vendored.

## DOCX functional reference — restricted source not incorporated

Sources identified by the incoming DOCX staging bundle and consulted only for
its public functional boundary:

- <https://github.com/K-Dense-AI/scientific-agent-skills/tree/b2a92ba052dcae4ef48e850b9e31ebf75706be48/skills/docx>
- <https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/f6656c1256d5a8adfa37db9110046ef20bac644c/skills/docx>

The incoming bundle identified the K-Dense source as Anthropic's DOCX skill
version `2.1`, extended with a second runtime. Its bundled `LICENSE.txt` was
not MIT and stated restrictions on extraction, retention, copying, derivative
works, and distribution. Marking the skill internal would not have cured
those restrictions in this public repository. The staging bundle was
therefore treated only as a functional requirements input and removed after
review. No source prose, Python or Node script, XML template, XSD schema,
fixture, reference guide, or license text from that bundle was copied,
translated, vendored, or adapted into the registered skill.

`skills/tool/q-tool-document` is an original Quasar implementation. It uses a
runtime-neutral caller-owned delegation contract; independent standard-library
Python and Node backends; bounded DOCX/DOTX inspection, extraction, creation,
exact text replacement, comments, redlines, accepted-change materialization,
conversion, rendering, and package validation; safe ZIP/OPC mechanics;
explicit overwrite authorization; capability-based fallbacks; and
cross-runtime tests. It bundles no ISO schema corpus or third-party runtime
dependency. LibreOffice, Poppler, and other separately installed native tools
retain their own licenses and are not vendored.

## XLSX functional reference — restricted source not incorporated

Sources identified by the incoming XLSX staging bundle and consulted only for
its public functional boundary:

- <https://github.com/K-Dense-AI/scientific-agent-skills/tree/b2a92ba052dcae4ef48e850b9e31ebf75706be48/skills/xlsx>
- <https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/f6656c1256d5a8adfa37db9110046ef20bac644c/skills/xlsx>

The incoming bundle contained Anthropic's non-MIT `LICENSE.txt`, with
restrictions on extraction, retention, copying, derivative works, distribution,
transfer, and reverse engineering. It was therefore used only to enumerate a
public spreadsheet capability surface and was removed after review. No source
prose, Python or Node script, fixture, guide, or license text from that bundle
was copied, translated, vendored, or adapted into the registered skill.

`skills/tool/q-tool-spreadsheet` is an original Quasar implementation. It uses a
caller-owned request/result contract; operation-aware, capability-checked local
Python and Node routes; safe package inspection; distinct atomic outputs;
read-only handling for macro-enabled and template workbooks; explicit overwrite
authorization; no macro or external-connection execution; isolated
LibreOffice-based conversion, recalculation, and rendering when available; and
deterministic cross-runtime tests. Official openpyxl 3.1.5, ExcelJS 4.4.0, and
LibreOffice command-line documentation were consulted for runtime behavior.
The Node dependency metadata overrides ExcelJS's transitive UUID package to
11.1.1 after an npm audit of the unmodified tree reported
[GHSA-w5hq-g745-h8pq](https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-w5hq-g745-h8pq);
the overridden temporary tree reported zero known vulnerabilities on
2026-08-14. Those separately installed dependencies retain their own licenses
and are not vendored.

## PPTX functional reference — restricted source not incorporated

Sources identified by the incoming PPTX implementation plan and preliminary
bundle, and consulted only for their public functional boundary:

- <https://github.com/K-Dense-AI/scientific-agent-skills/tree/b2a92ba052dcae4ef48e850b9e31ebf75706be48/skills/pptx>
- <https://github.com/anthropics/skills/tree/f6656c1256d5a8adfa37db9110046ef20bac644c/skills/pptx>

The incoming plan identified the source as Anthropic-derived and subject to a
non-MIT license with restrictions on copying, retention, derivative works,
distribution, transfer, and reverse engineering. The bundle was therefore
treated only as a functional requirements input. No source prose, script,
fixture, guide, asset, or license text from those reference skills was copied,
translated, vendored, or adapted into the registered skill.

`skills/tool/q-tool-pptx` is an original Quasar implementation. It uses a
caller-owned request/result contract; operation-aware, capability-checked local
Python and Node routes; bounded untrusted ZIP/XML inspection; distinct atomic
outputs; read-only handling for POTX and PPSX; explicit overwrite authorization;
no macro, embedded-content, or external-relationship execution; isolated local
rendering when available; and deterministic cross-runtime tests. Separately
installed python-pptx, Pillow, JSZip, fast-xml-parser, LibreOffice,
and Poppler retain their own licenses and are not vendored.
PptxGenJS 4.0.1 was evaluated but is not declared or used: its transitive
`image-size` dependency was affected by `GHSA-w3rx-r6r6-pgpr` and
`GHSA-5p2g-fcmc-qvqq`, with no corrected `image-size` release available on
2026-08-14. The resulting Node dependency tree reported zero known
vulnerabilities in `npm audit --omit=dev` on that date.

